# npm's only blocker.
#
# The other two foreign package managers stop themselves: pnpm and yarn both
# read `packageManager` from package.json and refuse ("This project is
# configured to use bun" / a demand for Corepack). npm honours that field only
# through Corepack, which is off by default — so without this flag npm is the
# one tool that would resolve, install and write a `package-lock.json`.
#
# `engine-strict` makes npm enforce the unsatisfiable `engines` ranges in
# package.json instead of warning about them. It is checked during RESOLUTION,
# before any lifecycle script, which is what makes it the only layer that
# survives `--ignore-scripts` — measured: `npm install --ignore-scripts` exits 1
# with `EBADENGINE` and prints the range verbatim, which is why the range reads
# as an instruction rather than a version.
#
# Bun ignores both this file's `engine-strict` and the `engines` field entirely
# (verified on 1.4.0). That is what makes the pair safe to keep here: it can only
# ever reject the tools this project does not use.
engine-strict=true
